// Q-068: the Logger and Metrics runtime sections were hand-written WAT from
// the era when a Mere value was an i32. Their signatures said i64 and their
// bodies said i32, so the module did not validate -- and once it did, the
// Logger record still used 4-byte fields while a record FIELD is read with
// `i64.load offset=(8 * idx)`, so `warn` returned the `error` closure and
// `error` read past the end.
//
// Nothing caught it because no Wasm-compiled program in the corpus used a
// capability. examples/toy_sql.mere does, and it had not lexed since the
// interpolation rule changed -- a program that does not lex is examined by no
// backend at all.
//
// Both widths are exercised on purpose: the record's 8-byte fields, and the
// closure's two i32 slots (env, fn_idx) that each field points at.
let logger = mk_logger "app";
let metrics = mk_metrics ();
{
  logger.info "hello";
  logger.warn "careful";
  logger.error "bad";
  metrics.inc "hits";
  metrics.record "latency" 42
}
